I was thinking a bit, too.  (Hard work for a samurai....)  Your suggestions
about the gems and potions are marvelous, though of course the dev team would
have to give its blessing to all of this.

I would recommend against the alchemist; just give (limited) potion
identification ability to the healer.  Archeologists already have gem ID.
Don't give any class special mixing abilities; make them all suffer.

However, my biggest insight is regarding the ingredients.  In AD&D, spells take
a material component as well as the blabberings and gesticulations, and
wizards and priests can make potions and scrolls (though the methods employed
by the two classes are quite disparate).  The common element for all of this
is the inclusion in many instances of a bit of some fabulous and rare beast.
For example, water walking might require ichor of giant eel, invisibility
may need the blood of a stalker, monster detection could require the lens of a
floating eye, and so on.

Eye of newt and wing of bat, anyone?
